  +--------------------+------------+------------------------------+
  | record_id          | String     | Unique ID for each record    |
  | age_group          | String     | 18-25, 26-35, 36-45,         |
  |                    |            | 46-60, 60+                   |
  | age                | Integer    | Age of the individual        |
  | weight             | Integer    | Weight in kilograms          |
  | gender             | String     | Male / Female / Other        |
  | region             | String     | North / South / East / West  |
  | smoking_status     | String     | Smoker / Non-Smoker /        |
  |                    |            | Former Smoker                |
  | exercise_frequency | String     | Daily / Weekly /             |
  |                    |            | Rarely / Never               |
  | bmi                | Float      | Body Mass Index              |
  | blood_pressure     | Float      | Systolic BP in mmHg          |
  | diabetes           | Boolean    | True / False                 |
  | hypertension       | Boolean    | True / False                 |
  | cholesterol_level  | Float      | Total cholesterol (mg/dL)    |
  | glucose_level      | Float      | Fasting glucose (mg/dL)      |
  | visit_date         | Date       | Date of health check-up      |
  +--------------------+------------+------------------------------+


================================================================
  PART A — THEORETICAL FOUNDATION (SHORT NOTES)
================================================================

  This section covers 8 core statistical concepts required to
  understand and interpret the analysis performed in Part B.

----------------------------------------------------------------
  1. INFERENTIAL STATISTICS
----------------------------------------------------------------

  Definition:
    Inferential statistics is the branch of statistics that uses
    data collected from a SAMPLE to make predictions, estimates,
    or decisions about a larger POPULATION.

  Key Idea:
    Instead of studying every individual in a population (which
    is often impossible), we collect a smaller sample and use
    mathematical tools to draw general conclusions.

  What it allows us to do:
    - Test hypotheses about population parameters
    - Estimate values like population mean or proportion
    - Determine whether relationships between variables are
      statistically significant or just due to chance

  Example:
    From a sample of 50 patients, we can conclude whether
    smoking affects diabetes in the entire population.

  Difference from Descriptive Statistics:
    Descriptive statistics only SUMMARIZES the data (mean,
    median, mode). Inferential statistics goes further and
    makes PREDICTIONS or DECISIONS beyond the sample.


----------------------------------------------------------------
  2. HYPOTHESIS TESTING AND ITS COMPONENTS
----------------------------------------------------------------

  Definition:
    Hypothesis testing is a formal procedure used to decide
    whether there is enough statistical evidence in a sample
    to support or reject a specific claim about a population.

  Components:

    H0 - Null Hypothesis:
      The default assumption. It states there is NO effect,
      NO difference, or NO relationship.
      Example: "Smoking has no effect on Diabetes."

    H1 - Alternative Hypothesis:
      The claim we are trying to prove. It states there IS
      an effect, difference, or relationship.
      Example: "Smoking significantly affects Diabetes."

    Alpha (a) - Significance Level:
      The threshold probability at which we decide to reject
      H0. Commonly set at 0.05 (5%).
      If p-value <= 0.05, we reject H0.

    Test Statistic:
      A value calculated from sample data that is used to
      determine whether to reject H0.
      Examples: z-score, t-score, F-value, chi-square value.

    p-value:
      The probability of observing the test result (or more
      extreme) if H0 were true. Small p-value = strong
      evidence against H0.

  Decision Rule:
    p-value <= alpha (0.05)  -->  REJECT H0
    p-value >  alpha (0.05)  -->  FAIL TO REJECT H0


----------------------------------------------------------------
  3. CONFIDENCE INTERVAL AND CRITICAL VALUE
----------------------------------------------------------------

  Confidence Interval (CI):
    A range of values calculated from sample data that is
    likely to contain the true population parameter with a
    certain level of confidence.

    Formula (for mean):
      CI = mean +/- (critical value * standard error)

    Interpretation:
      A 95% CI means: if we repeated the study 100 times,
      95 of the intervals would contain the true mean.

    Example from this project:
      Age  --> 95% CI: [38.03, 47.81]
      This means we are 95% confident the true average age
      of the population lies between 38 and 47.81 years.

  Critical Value:
    The boundary point on the distribution beyond which the
    test statistic must fall to reject H0.

    Common Critical Values:
      90% confidence  -->  z = +/- 1.645
      95% confidence  -->  z = +/- 1.960
      99% confidence  -->  z = +/- 2.576

    For t-distribution, the critical value depends on the
    degrees of freedom (df = n - 1).


----------------------------------------------------------------
  4. P-VALUE
----------------------------------------------------------------

  Definition:
    The p-value is the probability of obtaining a test result
    as extreme as the observed result, ASSUMING the null
    hypothesis (H0) is true.

  How to interpret:
    Small p-value (e.g., 0.001):
      Very unlikely under H0 --> Strong evidence to REJECT H0

    Large p-value (e.g., 0.45):
      Likely under H0 --> NOT enough evidence to reject H0

  Decision:
    p-value <= 0.05  -->  Statistically significant --> REJECT H0
    p-value >  0.05  -->  Not significant --> FAIL TO REJECT H0

  Important Note:
    p-value does NOT tell you the probability that H0 is true.
    It only tells you how surprising your result is if H0
    were true.

  Example:
    In chi-square test (Smoking vs Diabetes):
    p-value = 0.0000 < 0.05 --> REJECT H0
    Conclusion: Smoking IS significantly associated with Diabetes.


----------------------------------------------------------------
  5. TYPE I AND TYPE II ERRORS
----------------------------------------------------------------

  When making a decision in hypothesis testing, two types of
  errors are possible:

  Type I Error (False Positive):
    Definition : Rejecting H0 when H0 is actually TRUE.
    Symbol     : Alpha (a)
    Also called: False Positive
    Example    : Concluding smoking causes diabetes when it
                 actually does not.
    Control    : Set a lower significance level (e.g., 0.01)

  Type II Error (False Negative):
    Definition : Failing to reject H0 when H1 is actually TRUE.
    Symbol     : Beta (b)
    Also called: False Negative
    Example    : Concluding smoking does NOT cause diabetes
                 when it actually does.
    Control    : Increase sample size

  Trade-off:
    Reducing the risk of Type I error (lowering alpha) increases
    the risk of Type II error, and vice versa.

  Summary Table:
    +--------------------+------------------+------------------+
    |                    | H0 is TRUE       | H0 is FALSE      |
    +--------------------+------------------+------------------+
    | REJECT H0          | TYPE I ERROR (a) | Correct Decision |
    | FAIL TO REJECT H0  | Correct Decision | TYPE II ERROR(b) |
    +--------------------+------------------+------------------+


----------------------------------------------------------------
  6. STATISTICAL TESTS (z, t, Chi-Square, ANOVA)
----------------------------------------------------------------

  Z-TEST:
    Purpose  : Test whether the sample mean differs from the
               population mean.
    Use when : Sample size n > 30 AND population standard
               deviation is known.
    Statistic: z = (sample mean - population mean) /
                   (population std / sqrt(n))
    Example  : Is the average BMI of this sample significantly
               different from the national average?

  T-TEST:
    Purpose  : Compare means between two groups.
    Use when : Sample size n < 30 OR population std is unknown.
    Types    :
      One-sample t-test   : Compare sample mean to known value
      Independent t-test  : Compare means of two separate groups
      Paired t-test       : Compare means of same group at
                            two different times
    Used in this project :
      Comparing BMI of Smokers vs Non-Smokers (Task 4)

  CHI-SQUARE TEST:
    Purpose  : Test association between two categorical variables.
    Use when : Data is in the form of frequencies/counts in a
               contingency table.
    Statistic: chi2 = sum((observed - expected)^2 / expected)
    Used in this project:
      Smoking Status vs Diabetes (Task 5)

  ANOVA (Analysis of Variance):
    Purpose  : Compare means across THREE or more groups.
    Use when : You have one continuous variable and one
               categorical variable with 3+ groups.
    Statistic: F = variance between groups / variance within
    Used in this project:
      Age Group vs Glucose Level (Task 6)
    Note     : ANOVA tells IF a difference exists, not WHERE.
               Use post-hoc tests (e.g., Tukey) to find which
               groups differ.


----------------------------------------------------------------
  7. COVARIANCE
----------------------------------------------------------------

  Definition:
    Covariance measures how two variables change TOGETHER.
    It tells us the direction of the linear relationship
    between two continuous variables.

  Formula:
    Cov(X, Y) = sum((Xi - X_mean) * (Yi - Y_mean)) / (n - 1)

  Interpretation:
    Positive covariance : Both variables increase together
    Negative covariance : One increases, other decreases
    Zero covariance     : No linear relationship

  Limitation:
    Covariance is SCALE-DEPENDENT. Its value depends on the
    units of measurement, making it hard to compare across
    different datasets or variable pairs.

  Example from this project:
    Cov(Age, BMI)               = 42.8965
    Cov(BMI, Blood Pressure)    = 50.0549
    Cov(Glucose, Cholesterol)   = 1167.75

    These values cannot be directly compared because they are
    measured in different units.


----------------------------------------------------------------
  8. CORRELATION
----------------------------------------------------------------

  Definition:
    Correlation is the STANDARDIZED form of covariance. It
    measures the strength AND direction of the linear
    relationship between two variables, on a scale of -1 to +1.

  Formula (Pearson):
    r = Cov(X, Y) / (std_X * std_Y)

  Interpretation:
    r = +1.0         : Perfect positive linear relationship
    r = +0.7 to +1.0 : Strong positive relationship
    r = +0.4 to +0.7 : Moderate positive relationship
    r = 0.0          : No linear relationship
    r = -0.4 to -0.7 : Moderate negative relationship
    r = -1.0         : Perfect negative linear relationship

  Advantage over Covariance:
    Correlation is SCALE-INDEPENDENT. Values always fall
    between -1 and +1, making comparison easy.

  Results from this project:
    Age vs BMI             : r = 0.6824  (Moderate-Strong)
    BMI vs Blood Pressure  : r = 0.7667  (Strong)
    Glucose vs Cholesterol : r = 0.9935  (Very Strong)


================================================================
  PART B — DATA ANALYSIS & TESTING TASKS
================================================================

  All tasks are implemented in Derivable_Judgement.ipynb using
  Python with Pandas, NumPy, and SciPy libraries.

----------------------------------------------------------------
  TASK 1 — FORMULATE HYPOTHESES
----------------------------------------------------------------

  Hypothesis Set 1 : Smoking and Diabetes
  -----------------------------------------
    H0 : Smoking has NO effect on Diabetes prevalence.
    H1 : Smoking SIGNIFICANTLY affects Diabetes prevalence.
    Test to be used : Chi-Square Test

  Hypothesis Set 2 : Age Group and Glucose Level
  ------------------------------------------------
    H0 : Age group has NO significant effect on Glucose level.
    H1 : Older age groups have SIGNIFICANTLY higher Glucose.
    Test to be used : One-Way ANOVA


----------------------------------------------------------------
  TASK 2 — CONFIDENCE INTERVALS
----------------------------------------------------------------

  Calculated 95% Confidence Intervals for key numerical
  variables using the t-distribution formula.

  Method:
    CI = mean +/- t_critical * (std / sqrt(n))

  Results:
    +----------+--------+-----------+-----------+
    | Variable | Mean   | CI Lower  | CI Upper  |
    +----------+--------+-----------+-----------+
    | Age      | 42.92  | 38.03     | 47.81     |
    | Weight   | 76.52  | 73.45     | 79.59     |
    | BMI      | 26.53  | 25.37     | 27.69     |
    +----------+--------+-----------+-----------+

  Interpretation:
    We are 95% confident that the true average Age of the
    population lies between 38.03 and 47.81 years.


----------------------------------------------------------------
  TASK 3 & 4 — CRITICAL VALUE, P-VALUE AND T-TEST
----------------------------------------------------------------

  Test Performed : Independent Samples T-Test
  Variables      : BMI of Smokers vs BMI of Non-Smokers

  Purpose:
    To check whether there is a statistically significant
    difference in BMI between Smokers and Non-Smokers.

  Groups:
    Smokers     : n = 15,  Mean BMI = 31.38
    Non-Smokers : n = 24,  Mean BMI = 21.73

  Results:
    T-Statistic    : Calculated at runtime
    Critical Value : +/- 2.0211
    P-Value        : < 0.05

  Decision:
    REJECT H0

  Interpretation:
    There IS a statistically significant difference in BMI
    between Smokers and Non-Smokers. Smokers tend to have
    a higher BMI than Non-Smokers in this dataset.


----------------------------------------------------------------
  TASK 5 — CHI-SQUARE TEST
----------------------------------------------------------------

  Test Performed : Chi-Square Test of Independence
  Variables      : Smoking Status vs Diabetes (True/False)

  Purpose:
    To determine whether smoking status and diabetes diagnosis
    are statistically associated or independent.

  Contingency Table (observed frequencies):
    +---------------+-------+------+
    | Smoking       | False | True |
    +---------------+-------+------+
    | Former Smoker |   2   |   6  |
    | Non-Smoker    |  20   |   4  |
    | Smoker        |   3   |  15  |
    +---------------+-------+------+

  Results:
    Chi-Square Statistic : 20.6667
    Degrees of Freedom   : 2
    Critical Value       : 5.9915
    P-Value              : 0.0000

  Decision:
    REJECT H0  (chi2 > critical value AND p < 0.05)

  Interpretation:
    Smoking status is SIGNIFICANTLY associated with Diabetes.
    Smokers and Former Smokers show much higher diabetes rates
    compared to Non-Smokers.


----------------------------------------------------------------
  TASK 6 — ONE-WAY ANOVA
----------------------------------------------------------------

  Test Performed : One-Way ANOVA (F-Test)
  Variables      : Age Group (5 groups) vs Glucose Level

  Purpose:
    To check whether mean glucose levels differ significantly
    across the five age groups.

  Group Mean Glucose Levels:
    +------------+-----------------+
    | Age Group  | Mean Glucose    |
    +------------+-----------------+
    | 18-25      |  83.50 mg/dL    |
    | 26-35      |  91.70 mg/dL    |
    | 36-45      | 112.90 mg/dL    |
    | 46-60      | 131.80 mg/dL    |
    | 60+        | 155.30 mg/dL    |
    +------------+-----------------+

  Results:
    F-Statistic  : 90.3385
    F-Critical   : 2.5787
    P-Value      : 0.0000

  Decision:
    REJECT H0  (F-stat > F-critical AND p < 0.05)

  Interpretation:
    Age groups differ SIGNIFICANTLY in glucose levels.
    There is a clear upward trend — glucose level increases
    consistently with age. The 60+ group has nearly DOUBLE
    the glucose level of the 18-25 group.


----------------------------------------------------------------
  TASK 7 — COVARIANCE AND CORRELATION
----------------------------------------------------------------

  Purpose:
    To measure the direction and strength of linear
    relationships between pairs of continuous variables.

  Results:
    +-------------------------+--------------+--------+--------+------------+
    | Variable Pair           | Covariance   |   r    |   p    | Strength   |
    +-------------------------+--------------+--------+--------+------------+
    | Age vs BMI              |    42.8965   | 0.6824 | 0.0000 | Moderate   |
    | BMI vs Blood Pressure   |    50.0549   | 0.7667 | 0.0000 | Strong     |
    | Glucose vs Cholesterol  |  1167.7510   | 0.9935 | 0.0000 | Very Strong|
    +-------------------------+--------------+--------+--------+------------+

  Interpretations:
    Age vs BMI (r = 0.68):
      As age increases, BMI tends to increase moderately.
      Older individuals in this dataset carry more body mass.

    BMI vs Blood Pressure (r = 0.77):
      Strong positive relationship. Higher BMI is strongly
      linked to higher systolic blood pressure.

    Glucose vs Cholesterol (r = 0.99):
      Near-perfect positive correlation. Individuals with
      high fasting glucose almost always have high cholesterol.
      This is a critical clinical finding.


----------------------------------------------------------------
  TASK 8 — FINAL RESULTS SUMMARY (ACCEPT / REJECT H0)
----------------------------------------------------------------

  +-------------+-------------------------------+-------+---------+------------------+
  | Test        | Variables                     | Alpha | p-value | Decision         |
  +-------------+-------------------------------+-------+---------+------------------+
  | T-Test      | Smoker vs Non-Smoker BMI      | 0.05  | < 0.05  | REJECT H0        |
  | Chi-Square  | Smoking Status vs Diabetes    | 0.05  | 0.0000  | REJECT H0        |
  | ANOVA       | Age Group vs Glucose Level    | 0.05  | 0.0000  | REJECT H0        |
  +-------------+-------------------------------+-------+---------+------------------+

  ALL THREE NULL HYPOTHESES WERE REJECTED AT ALPHA = 0.05.


================================================================
  KEY FINDINGS AND CONCLUSIONS
================================================================

  1. Smoking and BMI:
     Smokers have a significantly higher BMI than Non-Smokers.
     This confirms smoking is associated with unhealthy body
     weight metrics.

  2. Smoking and Diabetes:
     There is a statistically significant association between
     smoking status and diabetes diagnosis. Smokers show
     much higher rates of diabetes in this dataset.

  3. Age and Glucose Level:
     Glucose levels rise sharply with age. The 60+ group
     averages 155.30 mg/dL vs only 83.50 mg/dL in 18-25
     group — nearly double. Age is a strong predictor of
     elevated glucose.

  4. BMI and Blood Pressure:
     Strong positive correlation (r = 0.77). Higher BMI
     strongly predicts higher systolic blood pressure,
     confirming the well-known obesity-hypertension link.

  5. Glucose and Cholesterol:
     Near-perfect correlation (r = 0.9935). Patients with
     high glucose almost always exhibit high cholesterol,
     suggesting these conditions co-occur frequently and
     may share common risk factors.


